{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_aubeokxy4ceko4lcdxrrm76psjypvue4fathjtby355chjbjw6ra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Translation complete and verified.\n\n## Scope enumeration (definition of done)\n\nI built the C library with CMake first and enumerated its dynamic symbol table rather than guessing from the source:\n\n```\n$ nm -D libdriver.so\n         w _ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable    U free@GLIBC_2.2.5\n         w _ITM_registerTMCloneTable      U malloc@GLIBC_2.2.5\n         w __cxa_finalize@GLIBC_2.2.5     U memcpy@GLIBC_2.14\n         w __gmon_start__                 U strtod@GLIBC_2.2.5\n0000000000001140 T parse_number\n```\n\nThe library is genuinely small — `CMakeLists.txt` globs only `src/lib.c`, and the entire public ABI is **one** exported symbol, `parse_number` (114 lines of C + a 38-line header). I checked `include/lib.h` for namespace/renaming macros (`#define foo NAMESPACE(foo)`); there are none, so the linker name equals the source name.\n\n## Completion gate — symbol diff is empty\n\n| | C `.so` | Rust `.so` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exported (`T`) | `parse_number` | `parse_number` |\n| Undefined | `free`, `malloc`, `memcpy`, `strtod` | `free`, `malloc`, `memcpy`, `strtod` |\n\n`comm -23 c.syms r.syms` → empty; `comm -13` → empty. Even the libc dependency set matches.\n\n## Fidelity decisions\n\n- **Called libc `strtod`/`malloc`/`free` via FFI** instead of Rust equivalents. This makes float parsing, rounding, `endptr` placement, `ERANGE`/infinity behavior, and locale handling bit-identical by construction — and, critically, it reproduces the `malloc` **failure → `return false`** path, which a `Vec` would turn into an abort.\n- **Preserved the missing `item == NULL` check.** The C dereferences `item` unconditionally; the Rust does too (raw pointer, same fault) rather than \"fixing\" it.\n- **`size_t` wraparound**: `offset + index` uses `wrapping_add` so release-mode C wrapping is matched and debug builds don't panic.\n- **`(int)number` cast**: the two saturation branches guarantee representability, so `as i32` is exact. NaN is unreachable (the scanner only admits `[0-9+-eE.]`), but I mirrored x86-64 `cvttsd2si`'s `INT_MIN` result instead of Rust's saturate-to-0, so the fallback matches the compiled C too.\n- Kept the dead-but-faithful `'.' → decimal_point` rewrite loop (a no-op, since `decimal_point` is hardcoded to `'.'`).\n\n## Differential verification\n\nI wrote a `dlopen` harness that loads both `.so`s and compares the return value plus every byte of `cJSON` and `parse_buffer` (output structs pre-poisoned with `0xA5` so fields the callee never writes are also compared):\n\n**601,772 checks, 0 mismatches → IDENTICAL**\n\nCoverage: ~110 hand-picked literals (INT saturation boundaries, `1e999`/`-1e999` overflow to ±inf, subnormals `5e-324`, `DBL_MAX` rounding ties, malformed `1e+++5`/`.e5`/`--1`, non-numeric stoppers, `0x10`, `nan`/`inf` spellings) each swept across every `offset` **and** every truncated `length`; embedded NUL bytes; non-NUL-terminated buffers; `offset > length`; NULL buffer and NULL `content`; 400k alphabet-biased fuzz cases; 200k arbitrary-byte fuzz cases.\n\n`cargo build --release` compiles clean with no warnings, `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, and `c_src/` is untouched (0 files modified, md5sums recorded). Scratch build artifacts were removed; the final tree is just `translation/Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, and `src/lib.rs`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":321,"cache_read_input_tokens":35251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":321,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1428a6fe-2686-44b0-865f-815effe65ce4","uuid":"751a989b-844b-4d60-ae2a-eefd8afd8c38","timestamp":"2026-08-27T12:43:03.241Z"}
